WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.187
Now combine the four conditions.

00:00:02.187 --> 00:00:05.522
The first is an uncorrelated scalar average.

00:00:05.522 --> 00:00:08.392
The second is a correlated scalar count.

00:00:08.392 --> 00:00:11.157
The third is correlated EXISTS.

00:00:11.157 --> 00:00:14.517
The fourth is another correlated scalar count.

00:00:14.517 --> 00:00:17.967
Read the WHERE clause one condition at a time.

00:00:17.967 --> 00:00:24.902
Because each piece has already been tested, the complete query is easier to understand and trust.

00:00:24.902 --> 00:00:28.967
For this activity, make a prediction before you run anything.

00:00:28.967 --> 00:00:32.272
State what one output row should represent, which records you expect to remain,

00:00:32.272 --> 00:00:38.157
and what edge case is most likely to expose a mistake.

00:00:38.157 --> 00:00:42.452
Then run the SQL and compare the result with that prediction.

00:00:42.452 --> 00:00:49.177
If it does not match, debug the logic in small pieces instead of changing several clauses at once.

00:00:49.177 --> 00:00:53.112
This is the same habit you would use in a real analytics project:

00:00:53.112 --> 00:00:57.600
validate the meaning of the result, not only whether the statement executes.
